<td id="S4.T1.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">24.4%</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r">BEST-RQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> (ep=100)</th>
<td id="S4.T1.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r">7.1%</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">20.5%</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r">      + iter. relabeling (ep=100)</th>
<td id="S4.T1.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r">6.3%</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">18.6%</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.2.6" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.2.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r">      + iter. relabeling<sup id="S4.T1.2.6.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S4.T1.2.6.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">2</span></sup> (ep=100)</th>
<td id="S4.T1.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r">6.2%</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">18.7%</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.2.7" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.2.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r"><span id="S4.T1.2.7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">BiRQ (k=3, ep=100)</span></th>
<td id="S4.T1.2.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r">6.6%</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">19.1%</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.2.8" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.2.8.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r">      <span id="S4.T1.2.8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">BiRQ (ep=200)</span></th>
<td id="S4.T1.2.8.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r"><span id="S4.T1.2.8.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">6.1%</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.8.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T1.2.8.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">17.4%</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.2.9" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.2.9.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r">      <span id="S4.T1.2.9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">BiRQ (ep=300)</span></th>
<td id="S4.T1.2.9.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r"><span id="S4.T1.2.9.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">5.9%</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.9.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T1.2.9.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">17.2%</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.2.10" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.2.10.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" colspan="3"><span id="S4.T1.2.10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ablation Study: Intermediate Layer Choice <math id="S4.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math></span></th></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.2.11" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.2.11.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">BiRQ (ep100, k=2)</th>
<td id="S4.T1.2.11.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">7.4%</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.11.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">21.1%</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.2.12" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.2.12.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r">BiRQ (ep100, k=4)</th>
<td id="S4.T1.2.12.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r">6.4%</td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.12.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">17.9%</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.2.13" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.2.13.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" colspan="3"><span id="S4.T1.2.13.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Variant / Extended Settings</span></th></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.2.14" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.2.14.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">BiRQ-4CB (ep100, k=3)</th>
<td id="S4.T1.2.14.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T1.2.14.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">6.2%</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.2.14.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T1.2.14.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">16.3%</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>WERs of 5-layer 137M Conformer (C1),
fine-tuned on 100h Librispeech labeled logmel data. SSL pretraining of BEST-RQ and BiRQ is on 960h Librispeech.
<span id="S4.T1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Main comparison:</span> BiRQ versus the non-SSL supervised-only baseline, BEST-RQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>,
and a HuBERT-style iterative labeling refinement variant.
<span id="S4.T1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ablation study:</span> Effect of different intermediate layers <math id="S4.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>
for generating enhanced pseudo-labels.
<span id="S4.T1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Variant / extended Settings:</span> Use
multi-codebook configurations. Best results are highlighted in bold.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Datasets.</span>
We evaluate our method on multiple speech corpora. In one experiment we use the <span id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LibriSpeech</span> corpus <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, with 960 hours of unlabeled audio for SSL pretraining and the 100-hour labeled subset for fine-tuning and evaluation. Evaluation is reported on the official <span id="S4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">test-clean</span> and <span id="S4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">test-other</span> splits using word error rate (WER). In another experiment we use a subset of the <span id="S4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">YODAS</span> corpus <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>, consisting of 5,000 hours of randomly selected unlabeled conversational audio for SSL. Fine-tuning is performed on the <span id="S4.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AMI</span> meeting dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> using 150-hour labeled data, with WERs reported following <span id="S4.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ami-ihm</span> / <span id="S4.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ami-sdm</span> splits.
All speech signals have a 16 kHz sampling rate. The acoustic features are 80-dimensional log-Mel filter banks computed using a 25 ms window with a 10 ms shift.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Self-supervised pretraining.</span>
We pretrain BEST-RQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> baselines and BiRQ using the <span id="S4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conformer</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> encoder. We experiment with three configurations:
(<span id="S4.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">C1)</span> 5-layer, 1024-width, 8-head with attention window of 200 (137M parameters),
<span id="S4.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(C2)</span> 10-layer, 768-width, 6-head with full attention (155M parameters), and
<span id="S4.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(C3)</span> 10-layer, 1024-width, 8-head with attention window of 200 (275M parameters). For (C1), we also compare with a HuBERT-style multi-stage iterative labeling variant of BEST-RQ (denoted “+iter. relabeling”) where refined pseudo labels are generated offline using the representations from the intermediate layer (<math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>rd-layer) from the previous BEST-RQ model for training of current model for 100 epochs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Input acoustic features are log-mel spectrograms and are downsampled by stacking every two consecutive frames. Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, span masking is applied by randomly selecting <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\%</annotation></semantics></math> of the total frame length and masking spans of 20 frames (effectively 40 frames due to stacking). For BiRQ, the loss weights are set to <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{1}=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{1}=0.1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{2}=2.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2.4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{2}=2.4</annotation></semantics></math>, corresponding to a bilevel penalty parameter <math id="S4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=w_{2}/w_{1}=24" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>24</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=w_{2}/w_{1}=24</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The 137M and 155M models are trained for 100 epochs with batch sizes of 100 and 64,
respectively, using the 960-hour LibriSpeech <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> unlabeled audio dataset. Both models use the AdamW optimizer with learning rates of <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.
The 275M model is trained for 100 epochs with batch sizes of 128, on the 5,000-hour YODAS speech using a learning rate of <math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math> under otherwise identical settings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Supervised fine-tuning.</span>
The 137M and 155M models are fine-tuned on the 100-hour labeled subset of LibriSpeech, while the 275M model is fine-tuned on the 150-hour labeled speech of the AMI meeting dataset. Fine-tuning is performed using the CTC loss <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>. The Adam optimizer is employed with a 10-epoch linear warmup to a peak learning rate of 0.001, held for 10 epochs, followed by 10-epoch annealing with per-epoch decay of rate <math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Decoding of Librispeech is conducted using the <math id="S4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-gram external language model (LM) provided by the Librispeech dataset. Decoding of AMI meetings is conducted using an in-house interpolated 4-gram external LM.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">137M Model Results.</span>
Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ BiRQ: Bi-Level Self-Labeling Random Quantization for Self-Supervised Speech Recognition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows the performance of BiRQ compared to supervised baselines, BEST-RQ, and a HuBERT-style multi-stage iterative relabeling variant of BEST-RQ.
Our results demonstrate that BiRQ consistently surpasses the supervised-only baseline, BEST-RQ and its iterative relabeling variant, achieving <math id="S4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5.9\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5.9</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.9\%</annotation></semantics></math> WER on <span id="S4.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">test-clean</span> and <math id="S4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="17.2\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>17.2</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">17.2\%</annotation></semantics></math> on <span id="S4.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">test-other</span> after 300 epochs. This indicates that BiRQ attains the benefits of multiple iterative refinements within a single, unified bilevel formulation, while using only half the memory footprint compared to iterative relabeling.
Ablation studies show that <math id="S4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=3</annotation></semantics></math> yields the strongest results, supporting our rule of thumb that <math id="S4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\approx 0.7K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>0.7</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\approx 0.7K</annotation></semantics></math>.
In addition, using multiple codebooks (4 vs. 1) further improves performance, confirming the importance of pseudo-label quality and diversity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scaling to 155M and 275M.</span>
Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ BiRQ: Bi-Level Self-Labeling Random Quantization for Self-Supervised Speech Recognition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> reports results on a thinner but deeper 155M Conformer. BiRQ continues to deliver strong improvements, reducing WERs to <math id="S4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5.0\%/12.6\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>5.0</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mn>12.6</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.0\%/12.6\%</annotation></semantics></math> compared to <math id="S4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6.8\%/19.6\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>6.8</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mn>19.6</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6.8\%/19.6\%</annotation></semantics></math> for BEST-RQ. This confirms that BiRQ generalizes well to different architectural configurations. Moreover, this setup incorporates full attention, showing that simple but more powerful architectural adjustments, similar to increasing codebooks, can complement BiRQ.
In Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ BiRQ: Bi-Level Self-Labeling Random Quantization for Self-Supervised Speech Recognition" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, scaling further to a 275M Conformer pretrained on the 5k-hour YODAS dataset shows that BiRQ provides consistent gains, lowering <span id="S4.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ami-ihm/-sdm</span> WERs to <math id="S4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16.3\%/34.0\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>16.3</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mn>34.0</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16.3\%/34.0\%</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S4.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="18.4\%/37.2\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>18.4</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mn>37.2</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">18.4\%/37.2\%</annotation></semantics></math> with BEST-RQ.
These results confirm that BiRQ not only improves label quality at modest model sizes but also scales effectively to larger architectures and datasets, making it a versatile framework for self-supervised speech representation learning.</p>
</div>
